Abstract

The rapid emergence of autonomous AI agents, self-improving large language models, and decentralized multi-agent systems creates risks that exceed the capacity of conventional cybersecurity and reactive alignment techniques. We present GAIA Sentinel—a mathematically grounded, distributed, self-correcting AI governance architecture modeled on biological immune systems, control theory, and formal verification. The framework introduces Internet-scale AI sensing networks, emergent-behavior prediction engines, provably stable containment mechanisms, and decentralized oversight councils underpinned by Lyapunov stability analysis, active inference, partially observable Markov decision processes (POMDPs), graph dynamical systems, and game theory. This dissertation develops the theoretical foundations, design principles, and formal safety guarantees of GAIA Sentinel. We provide a complete mathematical treatment of the core components, including Lyapunov-based stability certification, free energy principles for threat modeling, POMDP belief-state filtering for hidden capability inference, spectral phase-transition detection for swarm emergence, incentive-compatible multi-agent oversight, and zero-knowledge cryptographic auditability. We address the principal feasibility challenges—global sensing under partial observability, the autoimmune risks of a planetary monitoring infrastructure, and the strategic equilibrium of containment itself—by proposing a human-AI constitutional interface and staged deployment roadmap. The ultimate goal is not a centralized control system but a provably safe, resilient planetary intelligence ecosystem in which human sovereignty and scientific openness are preserved alongside advanced AI.

